532
00:20:53.320 --> 00:21:00.220
And I want to point out that the Scranton Business Park went all out with decorations.

533
00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:01.200
They really did.

534
00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:03.640
I mean, there's a Happy Valentine's Day banner.

535
00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:06.340
There's three red Cupid shooting heart arrows.

536
00:21:06.620 --> 00:21:09.900
Big hearts with love messages all on the walls.

537
00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:13.980
There's like a vase with like little plastic hearty things on the counter.

538
00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:18.800
There's a giant heart on the elevator doors that opens when they get in.

539
00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:21.800
Who is decorating the Scranton Business Park?

540
00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:22.500
I have a theory.

541
00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:23.940
What's your theory?

542
00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.340
I think it's Phyllis.

543
00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:27.740
She's married to Bob Vance.

544
00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:29.240
He's one of the five families.

545
00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:33.440
You might be right, since she can't be the Dunder Mifflin party planner anymore.

546
00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:33.960
Yeah.

547
00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:36.500
She's like, Bob, I've got a bin of hearts and cupids.

548
00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:37.940
I can put them around the lobby.

549
00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:41.420
I love Phyllis's voice when she's talking to Bob.

550
00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:50.740
Well, we also find out that it is Jim and Pam's first and only Valentine's Day as fiancés.

551
00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:51.640
Mm-hmm.

552
00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:57.260
Did you see at two minutes, 41 seconds that Pam's got some real cute dangle pink earrings

553
00:21:57.260 --> 00:21:58.300
and a sassy ponytail?

554
00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:00.260
I love the sassy ponytail.

555
00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:00.860
Me too.

556
00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:02.900
I do love a sassy ponytail.

557
00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:06.720
Kelly has received a Valentine from a secret admirer.

558
00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:07.260
Mm-hmm.

559
00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:08.520
She opens it up.

560
00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:10.920
It says, roses are red, violets are blue.

561
00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:14.720
It's time for your dental cleaning and maybe a checkup too.

562
00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:18.920
I want to say that this resonated with me.

563
00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:27.140
I have been Kelly where I've gotten what looks like a personal holiday card.

564
00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:27.620
Mm-hmm.

565
00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:30.160
And then it's from like my dry cleaner.

566
00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:31.080
Yeah.

567
00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:31.780
You know what I mean?

568
00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:32.160
Mm-hmm.

569
00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:37.460
Or a Valentine's Day card that I think, oh, oh, or birthday.

570
00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:39.520
Do you ever get the birthday greetings?

571
00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:39.800
Yes.

572
00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:40.420
From like...

573
00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:43.460
From like, you get like a birthday greeting from your gynecologiSt.

574
00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:44.000
Right.

575
00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:44.900
Exactly.

576
00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:50.480
At Christmas, you know, a lot of businesses will send out like a company Christmas card.

577
00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:50.860
Yeah.

578
00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:54.280
I tape them right up with all my family and friends Christmas cards.

579
00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:58.460
It just tickles me to be like, oh, there's like my friend's family.

580
00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:02.900
They're so cute and there's my aunt and uncle and there's my air conditioning company.

581
00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:07.100
Right there with a little wreath over a giant air conditioning unit.

582
00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:07.640
Mm-hmm.

583
00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:10.240
It goes up on the wall, guys, if you send it to me.

584
00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:12.280
This is true about you.

585
00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:18.660
Should we talk about Pam's beautiful Valentine's Day floral arrangement?

586
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:19.820
Oh, we shall.

587
00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:22.360
And I'm putting on my glasses for this conversation.

588
00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:23.100
Wow.

589
00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:29.720
That Jim gave Pam.

590
00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.420
And now I'm about to be tacky.

591
00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:34.260
Oh, okay.

592
00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:35.220
Here it is.

593
00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:38.580
I had a friend growing up who worked at our local flower shop.

594
00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:44.620
She told me that carnations are the cheapest flower and they use them to fill out arrangements.

595
00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:47.720
And another cheap flower they use are daisies.

596
00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.840
So if you want to go cheap, you get carnations and daisies.

597
00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:55.540
Jim's Valentine flowers are all carnations and daisies, I'm just saying.

598
00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:57.540
I noticed it as well.

599
00:23:58.340 --> 00:23:59.840
So I looked in the script.

600
00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:07.000
I wanted to see if there was a script note about Jim's flower arrangement to Pam.

601
00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:11.440
I want to hear it because I have a theory that I will prove.

602
00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:13.060
I think I can prove it.

603
00:24:13.300 --> 00:24:13.580
Okay.

604
00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:15.800
Here is what the script said.

605
00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:17.040
There was a note.

606
00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:23.680
There is a small bouquet of non-rose flowers on Pam's desk.

607
00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:24.540
Okay.

608
00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:31.300
Well, I think Phil Shea from Props ran with this because later in the episode, when they

609
00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:36.440
go to the fancy restaurant with Bob and Phyllis, the same arrangement is on all the tables.

610
00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:37.240
Really?

611
00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:37.900
Yes.

612
00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:44.040
And if you look up cheap flowers for big events, carnations and daisies, if you have an event,

613
00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:47.200
like a wedding or whatever, they help fill out the arrangements.

614
00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:49.800
So I think Phil was like, okay, great.

615
00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:56.340
I can get this small bouquet of the same arrangement that I'm doing for the dinner at the restaurant.

616
00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:57.180
Yeah.

617
00:24:57.820 --> 00:25:02.700
So in Jim's defense, I don't know, maybe it helped out our props department.

618
00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:07.920
Well, I'll also have you know, in the same scene at 3 minutes and 17 seconds, my grocery

619
00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:08.700
list is gone.

620
00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:09.720
You took it down.

621
00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.700
So we obviously shot this on a different day.

622
00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:12.320
Yeah.

623
00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:13.460
And I've been shopping now.

624
00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:15.560
So in case you were worried, I got all my stuff.

625
00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:17.240
Michael does not like the flowers.

626
00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:17.680
No.

627
00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:18.420
He's not a fan.

628
00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:19.400
He puts them on the floor.

629
00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:19.700
He puts them on the floor.

630
00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:20.800
He doesn't want to see them.

631
00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:21.980
I was like, you know what?

632
00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:23.500
I get that he doesn't want to see them.

633
00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:27.700
The floor, couldn't he just like put them like on one of those tables behind her?

634
00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:28.240
I don't know.

635
00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:29.280
He wouldn't be able to see them.

636
00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:32.960
Well, he goes up to Jim and he says, I need to have a word with you.

637
00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:39.000
And he says that today is a very difficult day for many people in the office and that

638
00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:43.700
Pam and Jim's sexy vibes are creating a hostile work environment.

639
00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:46.160
This whole conversation made me laugh so hard.

640
00:25:46.280 --> 00:25:50.660
First of all, it's kind of in hushed tones, you know, and they're saying ridiculous things

641
00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:51.440
in hushed tones.

642
00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:52.980
That was always really hard for me.

643
00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:57.520
And when you were like, Michael, you do not get to be in our relationship.

644
00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:05.280
Lady, I wrote that down because I remember making a choice to say that as if it's not

645
00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:07.400
the first time I've had to explain it to Michael.

646
00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:07.820
It was really good.

647
00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:09.300
I wanted it to have history.

648
00:26:09.520 --> 00:26:09.960
It did.

649
00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:11.160
It really paid off.

650
00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:16.580
Well, we had a fan question from Maddie L who says, when Michael asks Jim to have a word

651
00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:20.200
with him, he starts to stand up as if he's going to head to Michael's office, but

652
00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:24.500
then awkwardly sits back down when Michael chooses to sit on Jim's desk instead.

653
00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:26.500
Was this improvised?

654
00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:27.920
I think it was.

655
00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:30.640
I looked in the script to see if there was any...

656
00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:31.820
Stage direction.

657
00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:32.400
Yeah.

658
00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:33.760
There wasn't.

659
00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.520
Except that it clearly says they don't move into Michael's office.

660
00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:37.760
They stay at Jim's desk.

661
00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:41.620
So I think that feels like something that John and Steve probably found in the moment.

662
00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:42.600
It's very funny.

663
00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:43.560
A hundred percent.

664
00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:46.440
And I feel like those guys, you know, we've talked about this a lot.

665
00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:49.440
By this time, everyone knew their characters so well.

666
00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:55.140
So those tops and bottoms of a scene, you could kind of play around a little bit and it looked

667
00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:56.400
like them just having fun.

668
00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.260
It's time to talk about Dwight.

669
00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:02.480
I feel like Dwight...

670
00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:04.800
How do I say this?

671
00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:09.480
I feel like this episode really shows so much of who Dwight is.

672
00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:15.440
There's deleted scenes where he talks about the blood mobile being a vampire magnet after dark.

673
00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:18.380
He's ready for anything and everything, you know?

674
00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:19.620
Yes.

675
00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:23.320
And he has a lot of stuff to share about how his body can do things.

676
00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:24.340
Yes.

677
00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:29.080
The nurse is trying to take blood, but she can't find a vein.

678
00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:30.760
Well, you know why?

679
00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:32.300
He's hidden it from her.

680
00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:35.660
And when he's ready, he's going to pop it on out for her.

681
00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:36.060
Yeah.

682
00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:42.440
Then he has a talking head where he tells us he can also retract his penis up into himself.

683
00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:42.880
Yeah.

684
00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:47.220
He says, I train my major blood vessels to retract into my body on command.

685
00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.360
Also, I can retract my penis up into itself.

686
00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:52.660
We had a fan question from Robin T.

687
00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:53.940
Robin, go.

688
00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:58.900
Dwight's lines about retracting his blood vessels and penis is one of my

689
00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:00.760
favorite quotes from the entire show.

690
00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:05.240
It is so absolutely absurd and hilarious and so very Dwight.

691
00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:08.120
I want to know everything about this line.

692
00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:09.880
Was it scripted?

693
00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:12.000
How hard was it to get through?

694
00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:16.680
I'll tell you, it was scripted, but this was an alternate talking head.

695
00:28:16.780 --> 00:28:19.220
This is not the one that appeared in our shooting draft.

696
00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:24.840
So, I wasn't there when they shot it, but I have to imagine it was very difficult to get through.

697
00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:28.040
Well, it's Michael's turn to give blood, guys.

698
00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.060
Wow, that's a big needle, he says.

699
00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:31.680
Are you squeamish around needles?

700
00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:33.480
I can give blood.

701
00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:37.040
I don't get faint or anything, but I don't like to watch it.

702
00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:37.660
Yeah.

703
00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:39.380
I don't like to see the needle go in my arm.

704
00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:44.340
I don't like to see the bag fill up, but I don't like it faint or weird around blood.

705
00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:45.240
Yeah, yeah.

706
00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:47.780
If someone's bleeding, it doesn't make me faint.

707
00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:48.720
Yeah, same, same.

708
00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:49.120
Okay.

709
00:28:49.700 --> 00:28:51.760
Michael, on the other hand, is very nervous.

710
00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:57.860
But luckily, there's a lovely young woman also giving blood, and they start joking around.

711
00:28:58.320 --> 00:28:58.860
Yes.

712
00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:02.100
We asked Katie about this scene.

713
00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:10.820
I wanted to know, did she do any research into the process of giving blood and all of that?

714
00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:12.200
And here's what she had to say.

715
00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:29.640
I didn't do any research for this role, but I didn't need to because my character was just nervous and pleasant, and I didn't have to dig super deep to access those emotions.

716
00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:46.260
But the funny, I do have a very funny anecdote about being nervous, or my character being nervous, is that my first line was me just laughing nervously and saying, I'm so sorry,

717
00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:47.360
I'm so nervous.

718
00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:56.540
And Steve kept cutting, and he would be like, oh, no, it's okay, we'll go again, we'll go again.

719
00:29:56.660 --> 00:30:00.100
And finally, I was just like, I think it's my line.

720
00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:03.320
I think that's what I'm supposed to say.

721
00:30:04.040 --> 00:30:05.860
But that has always made me laugh.

722
00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:09.140
And of course, yes, I was thrilled to work with Steve.

723
00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:18.920
To get that, to get the show was its own triumph, but then to have my scene be with him was just like, I was over the moon.

724
00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:25.200
I love that story about Steve, because he was such a caretaker like that.

725
00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:31.100
He would want our guest actors to feel really comfortable, and he'd be like, oh, no, it's okay, we'll start again.

726
00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:32.360
We'll wait until you're comfortable.

727
00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:34.480
I was like, no, it's actually my line.

728
00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:41.280
I would also be relieved if the first time I ever had to be in a scene with Steve, I got to just laugh nervously and say I was nervous.

729
00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:41.720
Yes.

730
00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:43.800
Because it would be so truthful.

731
00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:45.400
Such an honest moment.

732
00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:45.780
Yeah.

733
00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:48.000
There's a third person in this scene.

734
00:30:48.220 --> 00:30:49.240
It's the nurse.

735
00:30:49.460 --> 00:30:50.540
She was great.

736
00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:54.860
I wondered if she was a real bloodmobile nurse, since that was a real bloodmobile.

737
00:30:55.040 --> 00:30:58.460
Yeah, and she seemed kind of no-nonsense, like very comfortable there.

738
00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:00.440
Well, she was, in fact, an actress.

739
00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:02.240
Her name is Julie Ramallah.

740
00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:04.220
She has not been on Monk.

741
00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:05.500
Or Law and Order.

742
00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:11.000
But she was on several episodes of The Young and the Restless, as well as many movies and TV shows.

743
00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:13.920
Well, Michael's going to make the mistake of looking at his blood bag.

744
00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:15.420
He freaks out a little.

745
00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:17.300
And he says to her, tell me stories.

746
00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:17.820
Say something.

747
00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:18.600
Distract me.

748
00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:20.640
And she says he's cute.

749
00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:21.600
Mm-hmm.

750
00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:23.360
And he's, I think, so happy.

751
00:31:23.560 --> 00:31:28.800
And then he shares that he didn't eat for three days because he was so nervous about giving blood.

752
00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:30.100
And he passes out.

753
00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:30.920
Yes.

754
00:31:30.920 --> 00:31:32.120
We had a fan question.

755
00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:38.680
Many, many people wanted to know, how do we do these scenes of people giving blood?

756
00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:41.580
Some people wanted to know, did they actually give blood?

757
00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:42.200
No.

758
00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:43.240
Oh, my goodness.

759
00:31:43.360 --> 00:31:43.720
No.

760
00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:44.440
You know.

761
00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:45.400
Not in the episode.

762
00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:48.720
Well, yes, the next day, but not while we were filming.

763
00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:53.500
The next day with trained professionals and not actors pretending to take blood.

764
00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:53.940
Correct.

765
00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:57.820
I found out how they make fakie blood.

766
00:31:58.500 --> 00:31:58.980
Oh.

767
00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:00.880
Thanks to Randy Cordray.

768
00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:02.120
How do they do it?

769
00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:02.760
All right.

770
00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:09.300
It's a combination of water, food coloring, and you're not going to believe it.

771
00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:11.280
One more thing.

772
00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:12.480
Wait, wait, wait.

773
00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:15.980
You'll never guess.

774
00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:16.780
Wait, wait, wait.

775
00:32:16.820 --> 00:32:17.640
I want to try to guess.

776
00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:18.980
You can eat it.

777
00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:20.680
I thought you could eat it.

778
00:32:20.740 --> 00:32:21.980
I was going to say like Jell-O.

779
00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:23.500
Nestle's quick.

780
00:32:23.940 --> 00:32:24.500
Oh.

781
00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:25.560
